# Which command-line options change the language a context comes up with,
# rather than what a run does with it.
#
# An image of virtual memory carries the language it was written with, and
# it is read as the context is created. So an option that changes what the
# boot files build must be written into the image and compared on the way
# back, or a run wanting one language reads an image of another. Those
# options carry an XPOST_VM_IMAGE_CONFIG_ bit; the rest do not, because
# they change what a run does and not what it is.
#
# The point of writing it down is the option that arrives later. A new
# option that changes the language and gains no bit would leave images of
# two languages telling each other apart by nothing, and nothing else in
# the tree would say so. So every option is classified here, and one that
# is not is a failure rather than an omission.
#
# option            language  bit
--define            no        -
--device-param      no        -
--enable-dps        yes       XPOST_VM_IMAGE_CONFIG_DPS
--help              no        -
--include           no        -
--jobserver         no        -
--license           no        -
--no-graphics       yes       XPOST_VM_IMAGE_CONFIG_NO_GRAPHICS
--no-sandbox        no        -
--quiet             no        -
--trace             no        -
--verbose           no        -
--version           no        -
